The subprogram receives the array and the number n of elements to fill. In C an array parameter is passed by address, so the elements written inside the function are written in the caller's array. The array is declared with `MAX + 1` elements so that position k holds the number k (position 0 is not used); in Pascal the same array is `array[1..100] of integer`.

```c
#define MAX 100

/* Fills the array tab with the numbers from 1 to n (position k holds k) */
void initialise(int tab[], int n)
{
    int i;

    for (i = 1; i <= n; i++)
        tab[i] = i;
}
```

*Example:* for `n = 10`, the call `initialise(tab, 10)` gives `tab[1] = 1, tab[2] = 2, ..., tab[10] = 10`, the state of step 3 of the illustration. The loop runs n times, so the elements beyond position n are left unchanged.

*Marking:* header with the two parameters (1 mark), loop from 1 to n (1 mark), assignment `tab[i] = i` (1 mark), array of at least 100 elements (1 mark).

**Idea.** A number x is prime when no number between 2 and x - 1 divides it. The paper asks to test only the **non-zero** numbers of the array `tab` that are smaller than x: the zeros are numbers already eliminated (1 and the multiples of smaller primes), and a composite divisor would itself be a multiple of a prime that is also a divisor, so skipping the eliminated numbers loses nothing. The subprogram has only the parameter x, so `tab` is the **global** array of the program. It returns 1 (true) for a prime number and 0 (false) otherwise.

```c
/* Returns 1 if x is prime, 0 otherwise: x is prime when no NON-ZERO number
   smaller than x in the array tab divides it (the number 1 is already set to zero) */
int verifyprime(int x)
{
    int k;

    if (x < 2)
        return 0;
    for (k = 2; k < x; k++)
        if (tab[k] != 0 && x % tab[k] == 0)
            return 0;           /* a non-zero divisor was found */
    return 1;
}
```

**Trace** on the array of step 7 of the illustration (N = 10, after the multiples of 2 were set to zero): `tab = 0 2 3 0 5 0 7 0 9 0`.

- `verifyprime(9)`: k = 2, `tab[2] = 2` and 9 mod 2 = 1; k = 3, `tab[3] = 3` and 9 mod 3 = 0, so the function returns **0**: 9 is not prime.
- `verifyprime(7)`: k = 2, 3, 5 give the remainders 1, 1, 2 (`tab[4]` and `tab[6]` are zero and are skipped), so the function returns **1**: 7 is prime.
- `verifyprime(4)` returns 0 (2 divides 4), `verifyprime(2)` returns 1 (the loop does not run) and `verifyprime(1)` returns 0 (1 is not prime, test `x < 2`).

*Marking:* header with the parameter x and a result (1 mark), loop over the numbers less than x (1 mark), test that the array element is non-zero (1 mark), divisibility test with `%` (1 mark), correct return values 1 and 0 (1 mark).

The multiples of y are 2y, 3y, 4y, ... up to the last position n of the array. The loop therefore starts at 2y (y itself is excluded) and adds y at each step.

```c
/* Sets to zero all the multiples of y in tab (up to position n), except y itself */
void setzero(int tab[], int n, int y)
{
    int k;

    for (k = 2 * y; k <= n; k += y)
        tab[k] = 0;
}
```

*Examples* (N = 10, steps 7 and 9 of the illustration): `setzero(tab, 10, 2)` sets `tab[4]`, `tab[6]`, `tab[8]` and `tab[10]` to zero and leaves `tab[2] = 2`; then `setzero(tab, 10, 3)` sets `tab[6]` (already zero) and `tab[9]` to zero and leaves `tab[3] = 3`. The array becomes `0 2 3 0 5 0 7 0 0 0`. For y greater than n / 2 the loop does not run (2y is already beyond n), which is correct: such a number has no multiple in the array.

*Marking:* header with the three parameters (1 mark), loop starting at 2y so that y is excluded (1 mark), step y and stop at n (1 mark), assignment of zero (1 mark).

The subprogram scans the positions 1 to n and displays only the elements that are different from zero (the numbers that were not eliminated, that is the prime numbers). The version below prints ten numbers per line and the number of elements displayed.

```c
/* Displays the non-zero elements of tab up to position n, ten per line */
void doDisplay(int tab[], int n)
{
    int i, count = 0;

    for (i = 1; i <= n; i++)
        if (tab[i] != 0)
        {
            printf("%4d", tab[i]);
            count++;
            if (count % 10 == 0)
                printf("\n");
        }
    if (count % 10 != 0)
        printf("\n");
    printf("Number of primes found: %d\n", count);
}
```

*Example:* for `n = 10` and `tab = 0 2 3 0 5 0 7 0 0 0`, the call `doDisplay(tab, 10)` prints

```
   2   3   5   7
Number of primes found: 4
```

*Marking:* loop from 1 to n (1 mark), test `tab[i] != 0` (1 mark), display of the element (1 mark). The count and the layout are extras.

The main program follows the six steps of the algorithm: read N (and check it, because the array holds at most 100 numbers and the range must contain at least the number 2), fill the array, set 1 to zero, compute rootN, then for each p from 2 to rootN use `verifyprime(p)` to know whether p is prime and `setzero` to eliminate its multiples, and finally call `doDisplay`.

```c
int main(void)
{
    int n, rootN, p;

    do
    {
        printf("Enter N (2 to %d): ", MAX);
        if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1)
            return 1;           /* not a number: stop */
    } while (n < 2 || n > MAX);

    initialise(tab, n);         /* step 2: fill the array with 1..N */
    tab[1] = 0;                 /* step 3: 1 is not prime */
    rootN = (int) ceil(sqrt((double) n));   /* square root of N, rounded up */

    for (p = 2; p <= rootN; p++)
        if (verifyprime(p))     /* p is the next prime number */
            setzero(tab, n, p); /* step 4: set its multiples to zero */

    printf("Prime numbers between 1 and %d:\n", n);
    doDisplay(tab, n);          /* step 6 */
    return 0;
}
```

*Remarks.*

- `rootN = (int) ceil(sqrt((double) n))` is the square root of N rounded **up** (4 for N = 10, 5 for N = 20), as in the illustration. Rounding down would also give the correct list, because a composite number not larger than N always has a prime factor not larger than the square root of N.
- `verifyprime(p)` is true only for a prime p, so the multiples of 4, 6, 9... (numbers already eliminated or composite) are never processed. The test `tab[p] != 0` would give the same result.
- With `sqrt`, some compilers require the maths library when linking (`gcc ... -lm`).
- The `do ... while` loop repeats the question until N is between 2 and 100 (it protects the array); a non-numeric input stops the program.

*Marking:* reading and checking N (1 mark), initialisation and 1 set to zero (1 mark), computation of rootN and the loop on p with `verifyprime` and `setzero` (1 mark), display (1 mark).

